# TITLE: Comprehensive Time-Resolved Visualizations of MW–M31 
#        Dark Matter Halo Evolution During a Major Merger
#
# ABSTRACT
# --------
# The Milky Way–Andromeda (MW–M31) merger represents a prototypical 
# major galaxy merger, providing an exceptional laboratory to study 
# dark matter halo evolution under extreme dynamical conditions. 
# While numerical metrics such as mass-weighted inertia tensors, 
# convex hulls, velocity anisotropy profiles, and spin parameters 
# quantify halo structure and dynamics, static plots cannot fully 
# capture the continuous evolution of these systems. 
#
# We present a suite of modular, time-resolved visualizations (movies) 
# derived from high-resolution N-body snapshots that animate halo 
# density, shape, orbital structure, and rotational properties. 
# Each movie focuses on a distinct aspect of the halos, providing 
# both qualitative intuition and quantitative insight. This pipeline 
# complements conventional analysis by explicitly showing how tidal 
# forces, orbital mixing, and angular momentum redistribution progress 
# through merger stages.
#
# 1. INTRODUCTION
# ---------------
# 1.1 Scientific Motivation
# -------------------------
# Major mergers drastically alter halo structure. Dark matter halos 
# respond to tidal interactions, violent relaxation, and dynamical 
# friction. Traditional metrics—e.g., axis ratios (b/a, c/a), 
# triaxiality, or velocity dispersion—provide snapshots in time 
# but fail to convey continuous changes in particle orbits, tidal 
# features, and the emergence of streams. Movies of halo evolution 
# therefore provide a complementary tool to elucidate:
#
#   • The emergence and evolution of tidal streams
#   • Coupling between halo shape and orbital anisotropy
#   • Redistribution of angular momentum
#   • Relaxation of halo density post-coalescence
#
# 1.2 Literature Context
# ----------------------
# Prior studies (e.g., van der Marel et al. 2012; Bullock et al. 2001) 
# have quantified MW–M31 dynamics using axis ratios, triaxiality, 
# and spin parameters. However, the use of movies to directly 
# visualize these quantities over hundreds of snapshots remains 
# underexplored. This work provides an integrated 
# visualization pipeline suitable for further exploration in this regard.
#
# 2. MOVIE PIPELINE OVERVIEW
# --------------------------
# 2.1 Design Philosophy
# ---------------------
# • Modular: Each movie focuses on a single halo property (shape, 
#   density, velocity anisotropy, spin, or rotation), preventing 
#   frame clutter.
# • Scalable: Supports hundreds of snapshots and millions of particles.
# • Publication-ready: High-DPI MP4 files, labeled axes, titles, and 
#   legends.
# • Reproducible: Movies are generated from HDF5 metrics and raw 
#   snapshot data, ensuring full reproducibility.
#
# 2.2 Movies Generated
# --------------------
# 2.2.1 Axis Ratio Evolution Movie
#   • Animates b/a and c/a for both mass-weighted inertia tensor 
#     and convex hull.
#   • Reveals triaxiality evolution during pericentric passages 
#     and post-merger relaxation.
#   • Time complexity: O(N) per snapshot (inertia tensor), O(M log M) 
#     per snapshot (convex hull, M << N)
#
# 2.2.2 Volume Inflation Factor Movie
#   • Shows ratio of convex hull volume to ellipsoidal volume.
#   • Highlights tidal features and departures from ellipsoidal symmetry.
#   • Memory usage: preallocated arrays per snapshot, bins negligible.
#
# 2.2.3 Velocity Anisotropy Profile Movie
#   • Computes β(r) = 1 - σ_t^2 / (2 σ_r^2) in logarithmic radial bins.
#   • Reveals whether particle orbits are radial or tangential.
#   • Provides deeper dynamical insight than velocity dispersion alone.
#   • Complexity: O(N_particles * N_bins) per snapshot; memory scales 
#     with radial bin count.
#
# 2.2.4 Density Projection Movie
#   • 2D XY projection of halo particle positions.
#   • Computes logarithmic density maps via 2D histograms.
#   • Captures tidal streams, halo overlap, and merger morphology.
#   • Complexity: O(N) per snapshot; memory: bins^2.
#
# 2.2.5 Rotational Profile and Spin Parameter Movie
#   • Computes rotation curves, escape velocity profiles, specific 
#     angular momentum, and Bullock spin parameter λ'.
#   • Highlights redistribution of angular momentum during merger.
#   • Provides direct comparison of pre- and post-merger rotation states.
#
# 3. METHODOLOGICAL DETAILS
# -------------------------
# 3.1 Snapshot Handling
#   • Snapshots numbered 0–800.
#   • Each snapshot includes MW and M31 halo particle positions 
#     (x,y,z), velocities (vx,vy,vz), and masses (m).
#   • Particles are concatenated, and the system is centered 
#     using either MW COM or barycenter.
#
# 3.2 Radial Binning
#   • Logarithmic radial bins used for β(r), rotation curves, 
#     and escape velocity.
#   • Number of bins (typically 30–40) balances resolution and 
#     noise.
#
# 3.3 Vectorization and Optimization
#   • Inertia tensor: fully vectorized, reducing complexity from 
#     O(N*3^2) to O(N*3)
#   • Convex hull computations limited to hull vertices (M << N), 
#     O(M log M)
#   • Preallocated arrays for metrics to minimize memory overhead
#   • Movies generated frame-by-frame to avoid storing full 3D arrays 
#     across all snapshots.
#
# 3.4 Visualization Considerations
#   • Log scaling used in density maps to emphasize tidal features.
#   • β(r) plotted with radial axes logarithmic, y-axis bounded [-1,1].
#   • Rotational curves and spin parameters plotted with clear 
#     legends and units.
#   • Titles indicate snapshot number and epoch for easy cross-reference.
#
# 4. COMPUTATIONAL COSTS
# ----------------------
# • Time: Each movie scales linearly with particle number per snapshot.
# • Space: Memory usage dominated by 2D histogram grids or radial 
#   bin arrays; O(bins^2) or O(N_bins) per snapshot.
# • Parallelization optional via multiprocessing or joblib for large 
#   datasets; not required for standard workflow.
#
# 5. VALIDATION AND ROBUSTNESS
# ----------------------------
# • Bootstrapping of particle subsets to estimate metric uncertainty.
# • Comparison between inertia tensor and convex hull axis ratios.
# • Testing on synthetic ellipsoids to confirm accuracy of shape 
#   and volume metrics.
# • Multiple centering schemes (MW COM vs barycenter) tested for 
#   consistency.
#
# 6. SCIENTIFIC INSIGHTS
# ----------------------
# • Movies reveal tidal tails, streams, and halo overlap not visible 
#   in static plots.
# • Axis ratio and triaxiality movies directly show the progression 
#   of halo deformation during pericentric passages.
# • Velocity anisotropy highlights redistribution of radial and 
#   tangential orbits throughout the merger.
# • Density and rotational movies complement quantitative metrics, 
#   providing physical intuition about the system’s dynamical state.
#
# 7. EXTENSIONS AND FUTURE WORK
